{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a0d7d5bd4b55e532c","tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01XpsojyGjUhUU26RkXXt8Gm","description":"Write comprehensive payments system design interview answers file","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"Write a comprehensive markdown file for a payments-system system design interview prep topic covering Section 03: State Management & Transaction Lifecycle.\n\nOutput to: `/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/03-state-management-transaction-lifecycle.md`\n\nHere is the COMPLETE file content to write. Write every word exactly as specified — do not truncate, summarize, or skip any section. The file must be technically precise about Stripe's API.\n\n---\n\nThe file must have this exact structure and content:\n\n# Section 3: State Management & Transaction Lifecycle\n\nIntro paragraph, then ## Core with 7 answers, then ## Deep with 3 answers.\n\nEach answer follows:\n### Q: <question>\n**Problem framing:** ...\n**Approach:** ...\n**Tradeoffs:** ...\n\n---\n\nHere are ALL the questions and answers to write:\n\n## CORE Q1\nQuestion: A Stripe `PaymentIntent` moves through states: `requires_payment_method` → `requires_confirmation` → `requires_action` → `processing` → `succeeded` (or `requires_capture`, `canceled`). How does your frontend model this state machine, and how do you decide which UI to render at each transition?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: why modeling the PaymentIntent FSM matters (race conditions, showing wrong UI)\n- Approach: \n  - Map each PI status to a React component/view\n  - Include a stateDiagram-v2 Mermaid diagram showing all transitions\n  - Code snippet: a React switch/render function that takes `paymentIntent.status` and returns the correct UI component\n  - `requires_action` triggers stripe.handleNextAction() for 3DS\n  - `requires_capture` means auth-only, show \"Order confirmed, payment pending capture\" \n  - Polling strategy for `processing` state (status is async)\n- Tradeoffs: polling vs webhooks for status updates, why not trust only client-side status\n\n## CORE Q2\nQuestion: What is idempotency in the context of payment requests, and how do you implement it on the frontend? Specifically, if a user clicks \"Pay\" and the request times out before you receive a response, how do you safely retry without charging the user twice?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: non-idempotent retries cause double charges; explain the HTTP timeout ambiguity problem\n- Approach:\n  - Generate idempotency key with crypto.randomUUID(), store in sessionStorage keyed to cart/session ID\n  - Code snippet showing key generation and storage:\n    ```javascript\n    function getOrCreateIdempotencyKey(cartId) {\n      const storageKey = `idempotency_key_${cartId}`;\n      let key = sessionStorage.getItem(storageKey);\n      if (!key) {\n        key = crypto.randomUUID();\n        sessionStorage.setItem(storageKey, key);\n      }\n      return key;\n    }\n    ```\n  - Send key as `Idempotency-Key` header to your backend (which forwards to Stripe)\n  - Stripe deduplicates requests with the same idempotency key within 24 hours\n  - On timeout: retry with SAME key — server returns cached response if original succeeded\n  - After confirmed success: clear the key from sessionStorage so a new purchase generates a new key\n  - PaymentIntent approach: even better — reuse same PI client_secret, call confirmCardPayment again; PI is naturally idempotent because you're operating on a single resource\n- Tradeoffs: UUID vs hash-based keys, sessionStorage vs memory (tab close risk), 24-hour Stripe dedup window\n\n## CORE Q3\nQuestion: How do you handle optimistic UI in a payment flow? Unlike a social media \"like,\" a payment has real monetary consequence if the optimistic update is wrong. Where do you draw the line between responsiveness and correctness?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: optimistic UI improves perceived performance but payments have high consequence of incorrectness\n- Approach:\n  - Don't use full optimistic UI for payment confirmation — too risky\n  - DO use optimistic UI for low-stakes steps: adding item to cart, applying coupon code, address validation\n  - For the actual charge: use a \"pending\" intermediate state, not a \"success\" state\n  - Show a loading/processing state immediately on Pay click (disable button, show spinner) — this is UI responsiveness without claiming success\n  - Only transition to success after receiving confirmed server response (PI status = succeeded or requires_capture)\n  - For 3DS flows: show \"Verifying with your bank...\" during requires_action\n  - Rollback strategy: if payment fails, re-enable form with error message, preserve cart state\n  - Code pattern: optimistic for cart mutations (add/remove items), pessimistic for payment confirmation\n- Tradeoffs: full optimistic (bad for payments), full pessimistic (bad UX for all steps), hybrid by step risk level\n\n## CORE Q4\nQuestion: Describe how you would manage the state of a multi-step checkout form across a full page refresh. What should be persisted (to `sessionStorage`, a URL param, or a server-side cart session), and what should be intentionally discarded to avoid security issues?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: users accidentally refresh, browser crashes, back-button navigation; need to restore progress without security leaks\n- Approach: three-tier persistence model:\n  - URL params: current step (step=2), cart ID — shareable, bookmarkable, not sensitive\n  - sessionStorage: form field values (name, address), selected shipping method, applied coupon — survives refresh, dies on tab close, not shareable\n  - Server-side cart session (cookie): cart contents, pricing, applied discounts, PaymentIntent ID — authoritative source of truth\n  - NEVER persist: raw card numbers, CVV, full PAN — never touch these, Stripe Elements handles them in iframe\n  - NEVER persist: PaymentIntent client_secret in localStorage (XSS risk) — okay in memory or sessionStorage with caveats\n  - On refresh: rehydrate from sessionStorage for UX fields, re-fetch cart from server for prices (prices may have changed)\n  - Code snippet showing rehydration:\n    ```javascript\n    function rehydrateCheckoutState(cartId) {\n      const saved = sessionStorage.getItem(`checkout_${cartId}`);\n      const localState = saved ? JSON.parse(saved) : {};\n      // Always re-fetch cart from server for canonical price/inventory\n      return fetchCart(cartId).then(serverCart => ({\n        ...localState,\n        cart: serverCart, // server is authoritative for price\n      }));\n    }\n    ```\n  - Mermaid flowchart showing what goes where\n- Tradeoffs: localStorage vs sessionStorage (XSS, persistence lifetime), server-side sessions vs stateless\n\n## CORE Q5\nQuestion: What happens to an in-flight payment confirmation if the user's browser tab is closed or the device loses power during the `processing` state? How does your frontend handle re-entry — e.g., the user re-opens the tab or returns to the site 10 minutes later?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: processing state is async — charge may or may not have gone through; user returns with unknown state\n- Approach:\n  - Store PaymentIntent ID (not client_secret) in server-side session or a durable store keyed to user/cart\n  - On re-entry: detect \"pending payment\" state from server (endpoint like GET /cart/status returns { paymentIntentId, status })\n  - Retrieve PI status: call your backend → stripe.retrieve(paymentIntentId) → check status\n  - Or client-side: if you have client_secret in sessionStorage, call stripe.retrievePaymentIntent(clientSecret)\n  - Status-based recovery:\n    - `succeeded` → show success page\n    - `requires_capture` → show success (order confirmed)\n    - `processing` → show \"still processing\" with auto-polling every 3s (max ~10 polls)\n    - `requires_action` → re-trigger stripe.handleNextAction(clientSecret) — user needs to complete 3DS\n    - `requires_payment_method` → payment failed (e.g., card declined during processing) → show retry form\n    - `canceled` → show failure, start fresh\n  - Code snippet for re-entry check:\n    ```javascript\n    async function handleReEntry(clientSecret) {\n      const { paymentIntent, error } = await stripe.retrievePaymentIntent(clientSecret);\n      if (error) return { state: 'error', error };\n      switch (paymentIntent.status) {\n        case 'succeeded': return { state: 'success', paymentIntent };\n        case 'processing': return { state: 'polling', paymentIntent };\n        case 'requires_action': return { state: 'action_required', paymentIntent };\n        case 'requires_payment_method': return { state: 'retry', paymentIntent };\n        default: return { state: 'unknown', paymentIntent };\n      }\n    }\n    ```\n  - Polling loop for `processing` with exponential backoff and max attempts\n- Tradeoffs: polling vs server-sent events vs webhook-driven push for status updates\n\n## CORE Q6\nQuestion: How do you handle concurrent modifications to a shared cart — e.g., the same user opens checkout in two browser tabs simultaneously? How do you detect the conflict on the frontend and recover gracefully?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: two tabs, one cart — race condition on mutations (add item, apply coupon, confirm payment)\n- Approach:\n  - Server-side: optimistic locking with cart version numbers (ETag or version field)\n  - Frontend: include version in mutation requests; server rejects stale version with 409 Conflict\n  - BroadcastChannel API for cross-tab communication in same browser:\n    ```javascript\n    const channel = new BroadcastChannel('checkout_cart');\n    channel.onmessage = (event) => {\n      if (event.data.type === 'CART_UPDATED') {\n        refetchCart(); // refresh from server\n      }\n      if (event.data.type === 'PAYMENT_INITIATED') {\n        // Another tab started checkout — disable this tab's pay button\n        setCartLocked(true);\n      }\n    };\n    // Broadcast when making mutations\n    function updateCart(item) {\n      return patchCart(item).then(result => {\n        channel.postMessage({ type: 'CART_UPDATED', cartId, version: result.version });\n        return result;\n      });\n    }\n    ```\n  - Payment initiation: broadcast PAYMENT_INITIATED so other tabs disable their checkout\n  - On conflict (409): re-fetch cart, show diff to user (\"Your cart was updated in another tab\"), let them proceed\n  - Last-write-wins vs first-write-wins for cart mutations; first-write-wins for payment (only one PI per cart)\n- Tradeoffs: BroadcastChannel (same browser only) vs shared server state, localStorage events as fallback\n\n## CORE Q7\nQuestion: A user completes payment but the success redirect is intercepted by an ad blocker or a browser extension that strips query params. How do you design the confirmation flow so the user reaches a correct success state even if your `?payment_intent=pi_xxx&redirect_status=succeeded` URL param never arrives?\n\nWrite ~500 words covering:\n- Problem framing: Stripe 3DS redirects return via URL params; ad blockers, privacy extensions, or corporate proxies can strip/block these; user lands on return_url without params\n- Approach:\n  - Don't rely solely on URL params for success determination\n  - Server-side: store PaymentIntent ID in the user's session before initiating payment\n  - On return URL load: check URL params first; if absent or redirect_status != 'succeeded', fall back to server check\n  - Backend endpoint: GET /checkout/status → looks up PI ID from session → calls Stripe retrieve → returns status\n  - Client-side fallback flow:\n    ```javascript\n    async function resolvePaymentStatus() {\n      const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);\n      const redirectStatus = params.get('redirect_status');\n      const clientSecret = params.get('payment_intent_client_secret');\n      \n      if (redirectStatus === 'succeeded' && clientSecret) {\n        // Happy path: params arrived intact\n        return { source: 'redirect_params', status: 'succeeded' };\n      }\n      \n      // Fallback: query server for canonical status\n      const response = await fetch('/api/checkout/status');\n      const { paymentIntentId, status } = await response.json();\n      return { source: 'server_poll', status };\n    }\n    ```\n  - Use stripe.retrievePaymentIntent(clientSecret) as secondary client-side fallback if you stored client_secret in sessionStorage\n  - Show a \"Confirming your payment...\" state while resolving, then branch to success/failure\n  - Webhooks as the canonical source: server should already know the outcome via webhook before user returns\n  - Mermaid flowchart of the resolution logic\n- Tradeoffs: relying on redirects alone (fragile), always-server-check (adds latency), hybrid (correct)\n\n---\n\n## DEEP Q1\nQuestion: Walk me through implementing a robust payment retry loop on the frontend. The user's card was declined (soft decline). You want to: (1) allow the user to update their card details, (2) re-confirm the same `PaymentIntent` rather than creating a new one, and (3) cap retries at a safe limit to avoid locking the card. How do you manage the state transitions, and what data do you pass back to the `confirmCardPayment` call on a retry?\n\nWrite ~750 words covering:\n- Problem framing: soft declines (insufficient_funds, do_not_honor, card_declined with decline_code) are retriable; hard declines (fraudulent, lost_card, stolen_card) are not; need to distinguish and manage retry state\n- Approach:\n  - Stripe error taxonomy: `error.type === 'card_error'`, check `error.code` and `error.decline_code`\n  - Soft decline codes: `insufficient_funds`, `do_not_honor`, `card_velocity_exceeded`, `try_again_later`\n  - Hard decline codes (do NOT retry): `card_declined` with decline_code `fraudulent`, `lost_card`, `stolen_card`, `pickup_card`; also `authentication_required` with no 3DS = hard\n  - Retry state machine:\n    ```javascript\n    const RETRY_CONFIG = {\n      maxAttempts: 3,\n      softDeclineCodes: new Set(['insufficient_funds', 'do_not_honor', 'card_velocity_exceeded']),\n      hardDeclineCodes: new Set(['fraudulent', 'lost_card', 'stolen_card', 'pickup_card']),\n    };\n    \n    const retryState = {\n      attempts: 0,\n      paymentIntentId: null, // reuse same PI\n      clientSecret: null,\n      lastDeclineCode: null,\n    };\n    ```\n  - Reusing the same PaymentIntent: PI moves back to `requires_payment_method` after a failed attempt; call `stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, { payment_method: newCardElement })` with updated payment method\n  - Code snippet for the full retry loop:\n    ```javascript\n    async function attemptPayment(clientSecret, cardElement, retryState) {\n      if (retryState.attempts >= RETRY_CONFIG.maxAttempts) {\n        return { success: false, reason: 'max_retries_exceeded' };\n      }\n      \n      const { paymentIntent, error } = await stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, {\n        payment_method: { card: cardElement },\n      });\n      \n      if (!error && paymentIntent.status === 'succeeded') {\n        return { success: true, paymentIntent };\n      }\n      \n      if (error?.type === 'card_error') {\n        const declineCode = error.decline_code || error.code;\n        retryState.lastDeclineCode = declineCode;\n        retryState.attempts++;\n        \n        if (RETRY_CONFIG.hardDeclineCodes.has(declineCode)) {\n          return { success: false, reason: 'hard_decline', error };\n        }\n        \n        if (RETRY_CONFIG.softDeclineCodes.has(declineCode)) {\n          // Allow user to update payment method and retry\n          return { success: false, reason: 'soft_decline', error, retriable: true };\n        }\n      }\n      \n      return { success: false, reason: 'other_error', error };\n    }\n    ```\n  - UI flow: on soft decline, show \"Card declined: [user-friendly message]. Please try a different card.\" — re-render Stripe Elements for new card input, keep same PI client_secret\n  - What to pass on retry: same `clientSecret` (same PI), new `cardElement` (updated payment method), optionally `payment_method_data` with billing details\n  - Retry counter: persist in component state + sessionStorage so refresh doesn't reset count\n  - After maxAttempts: advise user to contact bank, offer alternative payment methods (different card, PayPal), or create a new PI\n  - `requires_action` during retry: may need 3DS again — handle with stripe.handleNextAction()\n  - State transitions diagram (stateDiagram-v2): idle → confirming → declined (soft) → awaiting_new_card → confirming → ... → succeeded or max_retries\n- Tradeoffs: creating new PI vs reusing same PI (Stripe recommends reusing; avoids dangling PIs), retry delay (add 1-2s delay between retries to avoid rate limiting), user messaging\n\n## DEEP Q2\nQuestion: Describe how you would architect a frontend payment state machine using XState (or a comparable library). Define the states, events, guards, and side effects for the full lifecycle including 3DS authentication, network errors, and soft declines. What are the advantages of this approach over ad-hoc `if/else` logic in React component state?\n\nWrite ~800 words covering:\n- Problem framing: payment flows have complex conditional logic; ad-hoc state management leads to impossible states (showing success and error simultaneously), missed transitions, bugs from state explosion\n- Approach:\n  - XState v5 machine definition (use the new `createMachine` from xstate v5 style OR be explicit about v4 with `createMachine` + `assign`):\n  \n  Show a COMPLETE XState machine with these states:\n  - `idle` — initial\n  - `submitting` — calling confirmCardPayment\n  - `processing` — PI in processing state (polling)\n  - `requiresAction` — 3DS flow\n  - `softDeclined` — retriable error, awaiting new card\n  - `hardDeclined` — terminal failure\n  - `succeeded` — terminal success\n  - `networkError` — transient error with retry\n  \n  Events: `SUBMIT`, `PAYMENT_SUCCEEDED`, `SOFT_DECLINE`, `HARD_DECLINE`, `ACTION_REQUIRED`, `ACTION_COMPLETED`, `ACTION_FAILED`, `NETWORK_ERROR`, `RETRY`, `POLLING_SUCCESS`, `POLLING_FAILURE`, `MAX_RETRIES_REACHED`\n  \n  Guards: `canRetry` (retryCount < max), `isSoftDecline` (check decline code set), `isHardDecline`\n  \n  Show the full XState v4 machine config (since v4 is still widely used in interviews):\n  \n  ```javascript\n  import { createMachine, assign } from 'xstate';\n  \n  const paymentMachine = createMachine({\n    id: 'payment',\n    initial: 'idle',\n    context: {\n      clientSecret: null,\n      retryCount: 0,\n      maxRetries: 3,\n      error: null,\n      paymentIntent: null,\n      declineCode: null,\n    },\n    states: {\n      idle: {\n        on: {\n          SUBMIT: {\n            target: 'submitting',\n            actions: assign({ error: null }),\n          },\n        },\n      },\n      submitting: {\n        invoke: {\n          src: 'confirmPayment',\n          onDone: [\n            { target: 'succeeded', cond: 'isSucceeded', actions: assign({ paymentIntent: (_, e) => e.data }) },\n            { target: 'processing', cond: 'isProcessing' },\n            { target: 'requiresAction', cond: 'requiresAction' },\n          ],\n          onError: [\n            { target: 'softDeclined', cond: 'isSoftDecline', actions: ['assignError', 'incrementRetry'] },\n            { target: 'hardDeclined', cond: 'isHardDecline', actions: 'assignError' },\n            { target: 'networkError', actions: 'assignError' },\n          ],\n        },\n      },\n      requiresAction: {\n        invoke: {\n          src: 'handleNextAction',\n          onDone: { target: 'submitting' },\n          onError: { target: 'softDeclined', actions: 'assignError' },\n        },\n      },\n      processing: {\n        invoke: {\n          src: 'pollPaymentStatus',\n          onDone: [\n            { target: 'succeeded', cond: 'isSucceeded' },\n            { target: 'softDeclined', cond: 'isSoftDecline' },\n          ],\n          onError: { target: 'networkError', actions: 'assignError' },\n        },\n      },\n      softDeclined: {\n        on: {\n          RETRY: {\n            target: 'submitting',\n            cond: 'canRetry',\n          },\n          MAX_RETRIES_REACHED: 'hardDeclined',\n        },\n        always: {\n          target: 'hardDeclined',\n          cond: 'maxRetriesExceeded',\n        },\n      },\n      networkError: {\n        on: {\n          RETRY: 'submitting',\n        },\n      },\n      hardDeclined: { type: 'final' },\n      succeeded: { type: 'final' },\n    },\n  }, {\n    guards: {\n      canRetry: (ctx) => ctx.retryCount < ctx.maxRetries,\n      maxRetriesExceeded: (ctx) => ctx.retryCount >= ctx.maxRetries,\n      isSoftDecline: (_, e) => RETRY_CONFIG.softDeclineCodes.has(e.data?.decline_code),\n      isHardDecline: (_, e) => RETRY_CONFIG.hardDeclineCodes.has(e.data?.decline_code),\n      isSucceeded: (_, e) => e.data?.status === 'succeeded',\n      isProcessing: (_, e) => e.data?.status === 'processing',\n      requiresAction: (_, e) => e.data?.status === 'requires_action',\n    },\n    actions: {\n      assignError: assign({ error: (_, e) => e.data }),\n      incrementRetry: assign({ retryCount: (ctx) => ctx.retryCount + 1 }),\n    },\n    services: {\n      confirmPayment: (ctx) => stripe.confirmCardPayment(ctx.clientSecret, { payment_method: { card: cardElement } }),\n      handleNextAction: (ctx) => stripe.handleNextAction(ctx.clientSecret),\n      pollPaymentStatus: (ctx) => pollUntilResolved(ctx.clientSecret),\n    },\n  });\n  ```\n  \n  - React integration with `useMachine`:\n    ```javascript\n    const [state, send] = useMachine(paymentMachine, {\n      context: { clientSecret: props.clientSecret }\n    });\n    // Render based on state.value: 'idle' | 'submitting' | 'requiresAction' | etc.\n    ```\n  \n  - Advantages over ad-hoc state:\n    1. No impossible states — can't be in `succeeded` and `softDeclined` simultaneously\n    2. All transitions are explicit and documented\n    3. Side effects (services) are isolated and testable\n    4. State visualization with XState's inspector/visualizer\n    5. Guards prevent illegal transitions\n    6. Predictable and serializable context\n  \n  - Mermaid stateDiagram-v2 of the full machine\n\n- Tradeoffs: XState learning curve, bundle size (~15KB), overkill for simple flows; but justified for complex payment lifecycle with 3DS, retries, webhooks\n\n## DEEP Q3\nQuestion: Your checkout frontend runs inside a mobile WebView embedded in a native iOS/Android app. The native app and the WebView both need to know the payment outcome. Walk through the communication architecture: how does a payment confirmation event travel from the Stripe SDK (inside the WebView) through the `postMessage` bridge to the native layer, what are the failure modes, and how do you ensure exactly-once delivery of the success event to the native app?\n\nWrite ~800 words covering:\n- Problem framing: WebView isolation, need bidirectional communication, Stripe.js runs only in browser context; native needs outcome to update order state, navigate, show native success screen\n- Approach:\n  - Architecture overview (Mermaid flowchart):\n    - Stripe SDK → WebView JS → postMessage → Native WKWebView/WebView handler → Native app state\n  \n  - iOS (WKWebView): use `window.webkit.messageHandlers.paymentBridge.postMessage()`\n  - Android: use `window.PaymentBridge.postMessage()` (JavascriptInterface)\n  - Unified abstraction:\n    ```javascript\n    function postToNative(message) {\n      const payload = JSON.stringify(message);\n      if (window.webkit?.messageHandlers?.paymentBridge) {\n        // iOS\n        window.webkit.messageHandlers.paymentBridge.postMessage(payload);\n      } else if (window.PaymentBridge?.postMessage) {\n        // Android\n        window.PaymentBridge.postMessage(payload);\n      } else {\n        // Fallback: running in browser (dev/test)\n        console.warn('No native bridge found', message);\n      }\n    }\n    ```\n  \n  - Message schema:\n    ```javascript\n    const successMessage = {\n      type: 'PAYMENT_RESULT',\n      requestId: crypto.randomUUID(), // for dedup\n      status: 'succeeded', // | 'failed' | 'requires_action'\n      paymentIntentId: paymentIntent.id,\n      amount: paymentIntent.amount,\n      currency: paymentIntent.currency,\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    };\n    ```\n  \n  - Exactly-once delivery problem:\n    - postMessage is fire-and-forget; no built-in ACK\n    - Native may not receive if WebView is destroyed after postMessage\n    - Solution: ACK protocol\n      1. WebView sends message with `requestId`\n      2. Native sends ACK back via `webView.evaluateJavaScript(\"window.onNativeAck('requestId')\")`\n      3. WebView sets a timeout (e.g., 5s); if no ACK, retry postMessage (with same requestId for dedup on native)\n      4. Native deduplicates by requestId — stores seen IDs in a Set, ignores duplicates\n    \n    - Code for ACK tracking:\n      ```javascript\n      const pendingAcks = new Map(); // requestId → { message, retryCount, timeoutId }\n      \n      function sendWithAck(message, maxRetries = 3) {\n        const { requestId } = message;\n        \n        function attempt(retryCount) {\n          postToNative(message);\n          const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {\n            if (retryCount < maxRetries) {\n              attempt(retryCount + 1);\n            } else {\n              // Give up — fall back to server-side notification\n              triggerServerSideOutcome(message);\n            }\n          }, 3000 * Math.pow(2, retryCount)); // exponential backoff\n          \n          pendingAcks.set(requestId, { message, retryCount, timeoutId });\n        }\n        \n        attempt(0);\n      }\n      \n      window.onNativeAck = function(requestId) {\n        const pending = pendingAcks.get(requestId);\n        if (pending) {\n          clearTimeout(pending.timeoutId);\n          pendingAcks.delete(requestId);\n        }\n      };\n      ```\n  \n  - Failure modes and mitigations:\n    1. WebView destroyed before postMessage fires → store outcome in server session; native polls on resume\n    2. Bridge not initialized (race condition on app start) → queue messages and flush when bridge ready\n    3. Android JavascriptInterface not injected → feature-detect before sending, fallback to URL scheme\n    4. 3DS opens external browser (SFSafariViewController on iOS) → Stripe handles return via universal links, native intercepts, relaunches WebView with result\n    5. Message ordering: multiple events arrive out of order → include sequence number, native processes in order\n  \n  - Server-side fallback: native app polls `GET /api/orders/{orderId}/status` on app foreground (UIApplicationDidBecomeActiveNotification / onResume) — works even if bridge completely fails\n  \n  - Native listening side (pseudocode):\n    ```swift\n    // iOS WKScriptMessageHandler\n    func userContentController(_ userContentController: WKUserContentController,\n                               didReceive message: WKScriptMessage) {\n      guard let body = message.body as? String,\n            let data = body.data(using: .utf8),\n            let event = try? JSONDecoder().decode(PaymentEvent.self, from: data)\n      else { return }\n      \n      // Dedup\n      guard !processedIds.contains(event.requestId) else { return }\n      processedIds.insert(event.requestId)\n      \n      // ACK back to WebView\n      webView.evaluateJavaScript(\"window.onNativeAck('\\(event.requestId)')\")\n      \n      // Handle outcome\n      handlePaymentOutcome(event)\n    }\n    ```\n  \n  - Deep link / URL scheme as alternative channel: WebView navigates to `myapp://payment/success?pi=pi_xxx` — native intercepts navigation, handles outcome, never loads the URL. Simpler but unidirectional.\n  \n- Tradeoffs: postMessage+ACK (complex but reliable), URL scheme navigation (simple but limited data), shared server state (reliable but adds latency), native JSBridge frameworks (Cordova/Capacitor handle this boilerplate)\n\n---\n\nNow write the complete file. Start with the heading `# Section 3: State Management & Transaction Lifecycle`, then a brief intro paragraph, then `## Core` with all 7 questions, then `## Deep` with all 3 questions. Each question uses `### Q:` heading. Use proper markdown formatting throughout. Include all code blocks with proper language tags, all Mermaid diagrams, and make sure every answer is complete and not truncated.\n\nThe file should be roughly 6000-8000 words total. Write it all now to the specified path.\n","uuid":"869a60f1-ad5b-48c7-aee6-cc6099129f2a","session_id":"3b5e4a3c-6477-40c1-bb5a-cf9fbc849288"}
